#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125350} :activity/id #uuid "4535a3e9-8919-5e95-ab78-1b3e6bc5fe1d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125350 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125763 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis called a **basis** of an abstract vector space $V$ if the following two conditions hold:\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{B}$ spans $V.$\n\nFor example, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb R$ with degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\left\\{ {\\color{blue}{1}}, {\\color{blue}{t}}, {\\color{blue}{t^2}} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIs $\\mathcal{B}$ a basis for $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]?$ Let's check our two conditions.\n\n- First, we check for linear independence. Notice that the equation \n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} + x_2 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t} + x_3 \\cdot {\\color{blue}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\n \nis true for all $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\nx_1=x_2=x_3=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\n- Next, we check that $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$ Notice that we can write any polynomial $at^2+bt+c\\in\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ as a linear combination of vectors from $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nat^2+bt+c=c\\cdot{\\color{blue}1} + b\\cdot{\\color{blue}t} + a\\cdot{\\color{blue}t^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathcal B$ spans $\\mathbb{R}_2[t].$\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {1}, {t}, {t^2} \\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1d94c7bd-3777-5331-b633-0e2463092e26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3210 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b18f66cf-141c-5d9b-abc4-5dec05218f63" :step/math-academy-id 653375 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124302 :question/id #uuid "4b1227cb-7cf9-534d-a22f-e7acf7df9dc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4830"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447057}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "c37255e8-78c3-5483-a38d-706de2e319e3" :step/math-academy-id 653379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125764 :tutorial/content "A set of vectors $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ is a basis of a vector space $V$ if $\\mathcal{B}$ is a **maximally linearly independent set in $V$**. In other words, $\\mathcal{B}$ is linearly independent and for any vector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\in V,$ the set \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} \\cup \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\} = \\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{w}} \\}\n$$\n\nis linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc0c1b8c-4abf-5ce9-aac8-763330c5a699" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3211 :tutorial/title "An Alternative Definition"} :step/id #uuid "914841e5-976c-50b0-9edd-a7475085a0ff" :step/math-academy-id 653382 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125765 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ denote the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $\\mathbb{R}$ that have degree at most $n.$ Also, let $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ be the vector space of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$ Then, we have the following important standard bases.\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2 \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is $\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n \\}.$\n\n- The standard basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is\n![](images/11/1178fd622498d7466d7bddc4232ecc3cc9a9d9a8a9362766918069ba43110055.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd76fc85-4c43-564f-96ac-8492b69f8ecf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3212 :tutorial/title "Some Important Standard Bases"} :step/id #uuid "4e0810ce-4ac6-549b-8bdf-98dce31cb769" :step/math-academy-id 653386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124303 :question/id #uuid "91989c25-1d0d-5639-b3e1-7f7479322b7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4831"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447058}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "e09fa444-e077-5819-b997-b79f912a52f2" :step/math-academy-id 653383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125353}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124403 :question/id #uuid "92adb651-e2db-5296-817d-aea249afe2b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8269"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447056}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "df7f4408-7a22-5728-a508-8a07da995a75" :step/math-academy-id 653376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125351}}] :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124709}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406884} :activity/id #uuid "7a9a1a68-93b3-5d77-a9f3-56fa1ac555d7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552 :topic/id #uuid "a92f5097-81a3-51a2-90e4-397763d967f0" :topic/math-academy-id 1909} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4b0b94-d6cc-5fc8-aaef-54da3f8e617b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406856} {:answer/id #uuid "74ca9113-b90b-5028-9c16-d6040c62cabf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406862} {:answer/id #uuid "9276fe19-6956-5810-a884-2421c6c56cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406863} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f78152-7f85-5618-be57-9777140dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406865} {:answer/id #uuid "e4af5006-271e-5598-b528-2bbabe13d95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406872} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77beab8-8132-549d-89ed-5a3840170386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3557614-de80-516a-bb07-7b50ae99a56a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80631" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ are bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${x,{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-2{b}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{1})+{x}_{3}(5{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2{x}_{1}){b}_{3}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{1}+(5{x}_{3}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${-2{b}_{3},3{b}_{1},5{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{3}(3{b}_{1})+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}(5{b}_{2})+(-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2})(-2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "748c2276-dafd-5926-8dff-6ce428e10c5e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04483552-efe7-5ac0-9f49-b7cb22e5f5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406850} {:answer/id #uuid "154ae220-9109-5b3d-9009-7a90d7f05763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406852} {:answer/id #uuid "67110d56-fa92-5c33-8463-9dec2842fc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406861} {:answer/id #uuid "d33ae3a1-a6f3-5f6a-a11e-e64513528243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406869} {:answer/id #uuid "e179725d-36ab-557d-b57d-805d29bd2ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406861} :answer-field/id #uuid "21abb11f-a753-536d-8ed2-e4d2105529ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1d66ed66-f11f-53c8-af0e-1883d2bbdd40" :question/math-academy-id "q-94942" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of a vector space $V.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},w}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent\n- ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $V$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is a basis, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors in $V,$ and\n\n- $B$ spans $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $B$ is a basis of $V,$ it is a maximally linearly independent set of vectors. This means that if we add any new vector to the set, it becomes linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since if ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent then there exist ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere at least one of $x$'s is not zero. But then \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+0{b}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $B$ is linearly dependent. This would contradict the fact that $B$ is a basis.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}(3{b}_{2})+{x}_{3}(2{b}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(3{x}_{2}){b}_{2}+(2{x}_{3}){b}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},3{b}_{2},2{b}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}+{x}_{3}{b}_{3}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3}(3{b}_{2})+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{2}(2{b}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "aa1dd52d-ebd7-598e-b1e9-f1f6411b6354" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ad696f-46c1-5b25-95e1-0f62a7d8c499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406853} {:answer/id #uuid "36961107-cd5d-5ec8-9558-b9c722111836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406854} {:answer/id #uuid "5b6d00a5-b721-58e6-9958-8240023a0a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406859} {:answer/id #uuid "63e999ff-a2e3-5a48-9d48-fdfc510b1f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406860} {:answer/id #uuid "fcaf053d-057d-585d-bf1a-9d33a99b8261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406853} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf21969-de75-57e4-b536-3fbad5aa15ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94867" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "bc7cf94a-8206-5fe0-b854-5c6affbe827a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc69c43-a9f7-5f43-890e-96fe06c46748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406857} {:answer/id #uuid "5129302f-a0d3-5c48-9cd2-710367d9ef9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406858} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2afd25-1b47-58bd-a63a-fb16ca010eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406866} {:answer/id #uuid "e47c35f7-6343-5a83-8c29-d0f93c6a25ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406871} {:answer/id #uuid "fecfaf5f-f070-5827-a2e0-854b742a089f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406866} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0b26bf-4452-55f8-96b7-34adf0df266d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cceedad6-c377-5d6a-a68c-beab3237c2c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94866" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d8519c82-2015-5d4b-ab7d-68f0931d52be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d895ea-b8fa-5d9f-8273-d0bb03153342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406851} {:answer/id #uuid "46e97098-e4a4-5102-a43d-56b2c717a39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406855} {:answer/id #uuid "96a48ad5-c7b2-53c5-9bdb-aa723c9b9fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406864} {:answer/id #uuid "be96ccd4-bd92-5133-aad2-a91841ec919a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406867} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fdb3c3-35ae-5bf6-800c-8390e5189040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406867} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc08e474-6dee-51de-a67a-78ce180dd388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" :question/math-academy-id "q-107533" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-b)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-c)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "f5d3d190-875f-5a88-afc2-f8fcfd0bc6a4"}] :activity/title "Bases in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406849}]}
